\IfFileExists{ou3_lever_arm_cutoff.svg}{%
  \begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=\columnwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou3_lever_arm_cutoff}
    \caption{The deployable model's one design parameter.  Too narrow a
    derivative band and the low-pass phase lag misaligns a correction whose
    amplitude is already right, so the model is worse than none; too wide and
    the differentiated gyroscope noise it admits exceeds the term it removes.
    Between the two failure modes the basin is flat, which is what makes the
    deployable model robust rather than tuned.}
    \label{fig:imu-lever-arm-cutoff}
  \end{figure}
}{}

Two conclusions follow.  First, the installation penalty is real at realistic
offsets and it is not uniform across the state.  Attitude degrades well before
displacement does, and the two channels do not even share a worst case: the
displacement penalty peaks in the mildest seas, where the injected term is
largest relative to the wave signal, while the tilt penalty peaks in the
steepest ones.  A single pooled ratio therefore understates the effect in both
directions, which is why Fig.~\ref{fig:imu-lever-arm-sea-state} reports it sea
by sea.

Second, the penalty is entirely deterministic.  The exact-model arm returns to
the CG baseline in every direction, at every offset, and in every sea, so
nothing about an off-CG installation exceeds what \eqref{eq:imu-lever-arm}
describes; there is no residual stochastic cost to pay.  The gyro-model arm
shows that most of that recovery survives having only a noisy, biased rate to
differentiate, provided the derivative band is chosen inside the basin of
Fig.~\ref{fig:imu-lever-arm-cutoff}.  The practical consequence for an
installation is that the lever arm must be measured and modeled, not that the
IMU must be moved to the CG.

\subsection{Estimating the Lever Arm Instead of Measuring It}
\label{sec:imu-lever-arm-selfcal}

Both modeling arms above take $r$ as an input.  That is the honest reading of
the gyro-model result: it replaces the \emph{kinematics} with a reconstruction
from the measured rate, not the \emph{geometry}, and on a real vessel the
geometry is a tape measure plus an estimate of where the center of gravity
sits.  The obvious question is whether the estimator can supply $r$ itself.

Algebraically it can, and cheaply, because \eqref{eq:imu-lever-arm} is linear
in $r$:
%
\begin{equation}
  \dot{\omega}\times r + \omega\times(\omega\times r)
  = M(\omega,\dot{\omega})\, r,
  \qquad
  M = [\dot{\omega}]_\times + [\omega]_\times[\omega]_\times .
  \label{eq:imu-lever-arm-linear}
\end{equation}
%
The Jacobian of the injected term with respect to $r$ is therefore $M$ itself,
exactly, with no linearization error of its own.  OU--III can carry $r$ as three
more states in the same error-state vector that already carries attitude, the
gyroscope bias and the accelerometer bias, corrected by the same accelerometer
update, and read back with a covariance --- the lever arm stops being a
configuration constant and becomes a filter output.  The states are compiled in
only when asked for, so a build that supplies $r$ pays nothing for the option.

What \eqref{eq:imu-lever-arm-linear} also makes explicit is the limit.  $M$
annihilates any $r$ parallel to the instantaneous rotation axis: if $\omega$,
$\dot{\omega}$ and $r$ are collinear then both cross products vanish, and the
sensor at $r$ measures exactly what a sensor at the tracked point would.  A
vessel that rotates about one fixed axis can never reveal the component of $r$
along it, no matter how long it is observed.  A seaway is favourable here
because it rolls, pitches and yaws at once and the instantaneous axis wanders,
which is what makes all three components reachable --- but how vigorously it
wanders is a property of the sea state, not of the estimator.

That is what the measured behaviour shows.  In a synthetic rigid-body case with
rich three-axis rotation the estimator recovers a \SI{39}{cm} lever arm from a
zero prior to within a few millimetres, and does so with the latent OU
acceleration $a_w$ free to compete for the same accelerometer channel; the two
separate because the lever term is rigidly tied to a rate the filter also
measures, while $a_w$'s prior says it is unpredictable.  Restrict the same
scenario to a single rotation axis and the component along that axis is not
recovered at all, while the covariance correctly keeps essentially its whole
prior width there.

On the wave records the excitation is the binding constraint rather than the
algebra, and it scales with sea state exactly as
\eqref{eq:imu-lever-arm-linear} predicts.  Sea by sea, at \SI{30}{cm} and in
every canonical direction, the recovered fraction of the arm is monotone in
$H_s$: at \SI{0.27}{m} the estimator recovers essentially none of it, and at
\SI{8.5}{m} it recovers between roughly half and nine tenths of it depending on
direction.  The vertical arm is the best case throughout, because roll and
pitch are the rotations a seaway supplies most of, and they are the ones that
move a vertically offset sensor.

The consequence for the score is not a uniform improvement, and this is the
result that decides how the feature should be used.  A partial calibration
removes almost the whole attitude penalty --- the worst pooled tilt ratio at
\SI{30}{cm} falls from about \num{1.99} unmodeled to about \num{1.02} --- but
it makes displacement \emph{worse} than leaving the lever arm alone, the worst
3-D ratio rising from about \num{1.02} to about \num{1.11}.  A correction of
the wrong size, applied in a way that is correlated with the rotation, is worse
for a doubly integrated quantity than no correction at all.  The exact and
gyro-derived models do not have this failure mode because they are not guessing
at the size.

Fig.~\ref{fig:imu-lever-arm-calibration} reports the calibration itself rather
than the score, and its right-hand panel is the part that matters for anyone
tempted to use the number.  The estimate's error is set by model error ---
residual tilt, the accelerometer bias competing for the non-zero mean of the
centripetal term, and an $\dot{\omega}$ reconstructed by differentiating a
noisy rate --- and none of that is in the innovation covariance.  The filter
therefore reports a couple of millimetres of uncertainty on an estimate that is
tens of centimetres out, two orders of magnitude apart.  The covariance is a
correct statement about how well conditioned the regression is; it is not an
accuracy claim, and it must not be read as one.

\IfFileExists{ou3_lever_arm_calibration.svg}{%
  \begin{figure*}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=\textwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou3_lever_arm_calibration}
    \caption{The self-calibrating arm scored on its calibration rather than on
    the displacement it produces.  Left: the fraction of the installed lever
    arm the estimator recovers, against installation offset, per canonical
    direction.  Right: that error sea by sea at \SI{30}{cm}, drawn against the
    widest principal standard deviation the filter reports for it, for the
    direction the estimator does \emph{best} in --- the gap is wider still in
    the other two.  That gap is the finding: the estimate is limited by what
    the measurement model omits, not by measurement noise, so the covariance
    describes the conditioning of the regression and not the accuracy of its
    answer.}
    \label{fig:imu-lever-arm-calibration}
  \end{figure*}
}{}

The engineering conclusion is narrower than ``the filter calibrates itself'',
and it is a conclusion about the prior rather than about the algebra.  Seeded
with nothing, the estimator converges to a partial answer that helps attitude,
hurts displacement, and does not report its own remaining error --- three
reasons not to run it that way at sea.  Seeded with a measured $r$ and a prior
of a few centimetres, it does both halves of what a calibration has to do: a
survey entered \SI{10}{cm} wrong is pulled to within \SI{1}{cm} of truth over
ten minutes of three-axis rotation, and a survey entered correctly drifts by
\SI{2}{mm} over the same run rather than wandering off it.  That is the useful
mode --- a \emph{check on a survey}, not a replacement for one --- and the
motion that makes it work is the same lively multi-axis motion in which an
unmodeled lever arm costs the most.

Where the lever arm can be measured at all, the gyro-model arm of
Table~\ref{tab:imu-lever-arm} remains the better instrument.  It recovers the
whole penalty on both channels, it does so in every sea rather than only the
lively ones, and it does not have to discover the geometry to do it.  What the
estimated arm adds is not a way to skip the tape measure; it is a way to find
out that the tape measure was read wrong.
